<li about="#cite_note-VoltaCaio2015Quotation-9" id="cite_note-VoltaCaio2015Quotation-9" data-mw-footnote-number="9"><span class="mw-cite-backlink" id="mwBVE"><a href="./Gluten-free_diet#cite_ref-VoltaCaio2015Quotation_9-0" rel="mw:referencedBy" id="mwBVI"><span class="mw-linkback-text" id="mwBVM">↑</span></a></span> <span id="mw-reference-text-cite_note-VoltaCaio2015Quotation-9" class="mw-reference-text reference-text"><link rel="mw-deduplicated-inline-style" href="mw-data:TemplateStyles:r1333433106" about="#mwt24" typeof="mw:Extension/templatestyles mw:Transclusion" data-mw='{"name":"templatestyles","attrs":{"src":"Module:Citation/CS1/styles.css"},"body":{"extsrc":""},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"cite journal ","href":"./Template:Cite_journal"},"params":{"vauthors":{"wt":"Volta U, Caio G, De Giorgio R, Henriksen C, Skodje G, Lundin KE"},"title":{"wt":"Non-celiac gluten sensitivity: a work-in-progress entity in the spectrum of wheat-related disorders"},"journal":{"wt":"Best Practice &amp; Research. Clinical Gastroenterology"},"volume":{"wt":"29"},"issue":{"wt":"3"},"pages":{"wt":"477–91"},"date":{"wt":"June 2015"},"pmid":{"wt":"26060112"},"doi":{"wt":"10.1016/j.bpg.2015.04.006"},"quote":{"wt":"A recently proposed approach to NCGS diagnosis is an objective improvement of gastrointestinal symptoms and extra-intestinal manifestations assessed through a rating scale before and after GFD. Although a standardized symptom rating scale is not yet applied worldwide, a recent study indicated that a decrease of the global symptom score higher than 50% after GFD can be regarded as confirmatory of NCGS (Table 1) [53]. (…) After the confirmation of NCGS diagnosis, according to the previously mentioned work-up, patients are advized to start with a GFD [49]."}},"i":0}}]}' id="mwBVQ"/><cite id="CITEREFVoltaCaioDe_GiorgioHenriksen2015" class="citation journal cs1" about="#mwt24">Volta U, Caio G, De Giorgio R, Henriksen C, Skodje G, Lundin KE (June 2015). "Non-celiac gluten sensitivity: a work-in-progress entity in the spectrum of wheat-related disorders". <i id="mwBVU">Best Practice &amp; Research. Clinical Gastroenterology</i>. <b id="mwBVY">29</b> (3): <span class="nowrap" id="mwBVc">477–</span>91. <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="./Doi_(identifier)" title="Doi (identifier)" class="mw-redirect" id="mwBVg">doi</a>:<a rel="mw:ExtLink nofollow" href="https://doi.org/10.1016%2Fj.bpg.2015.04.006" class="external text" id="mwBVk">10.1016/j.bpg.2015.04.006</a>. <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="./PMID_(identifier)" title="PMID (identifier)" class="mw-redirect" id="mwBVo">PMID</a><span typeof="mw:Entity" id="mwBVs"> </span><a rel="mw:ExtLink nofollow" href="https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/26060112" class="external text" id="mwBVw">26060112</a>. <q id="mwBV0">A recently proposed approach to NCGS diagnosis is an objective improvement of gastrointestinal symptoms and extra-intestinal manifestations assessed through a rating scale before and after GFD. Although a standardized symptom rating scale is not yet applied worldwide, a recent study indicated that a decrease of the global symptom score higher than 50% after GFD can be regarded as confirmatory of NCGS (Table 1) [53]. (…) After the confirmation of NCGS diagnosis, according to the previously mentioned work-up, patients are advized to start with a GFD [49].</q></cite><span title="ctx_ver=Z39.88-2004&amp;rft_val_fmt=info%3Aofi%2Ffmt%3Akev%3Amtx%3Ajournal&amp;rft.genre=article&amp;rft.jtitle=Best+Practice+%26+Research.+Clinical+Gastroenterology&amp;rft.atitle=Non-celiac+gluten+sensitivity%3A+a+work-in-progress+entity+in+the+spectrum+of+wheat-related+disorders&amp;rft.volume=29&amp;rft.issue=3&amp;rft.pages=477-91&amp;rft.date=2015-06&amp;rft_id=info%3Adoi%2F10.1016%2Fj.bpg.2015.04.006&amp;rft_id=info%3Apmid%2F26060112&amp;rft.aulast=Volta&amp;rft.aufirst=U&amp;rft.au=Caio%2C+G&amp;rft.au=De+Giorgio%2C+R&amp;rft.au=Henriksen%2C+C&amp;rft.au=Skodje%2C+G&amp;rft.au=Lundin%2C+KE&amp;rfr_id=info%3Asid%2Fen.wikipedia.org%3AGluten-free+diet" class="Z3988" about="#mwt24" id="mwBV4"></span></span></li>
