<li about="#cite_note-MulderWanrooij-11" id="cite_note-MulderWanrooij-11" data-mw-footnote-number="11"><span rel="mw:referencedBy" class="mw-cite-backlink" id="mwBYA"><a href="./Gluten-free_diet#cite_ref-MulderWanrooij_11-0" id="mwBYE"><span class="mw-linkback-text" id="mwBYI">1</span></a> <a href="./Gluten-free_diet#cite_ref-MulderWanrooij_11-1" id="mwBYM"><span class="mw-linkback-text" id="mwBYQ">2</span></a> <a href="./Gluten-free_diet#cite_ref-MulderWanrooij_11-2" id="mwBYU"><span class="mw-linkback-text" id="mwBYY">3</span></a></span> <span id="mw-reference-text-cite_note-MulderWanrooij-11" class="mw-reference-text reference-text"><link rel="mw-deduplicated-inline-style" href="mw-data:TemplateStyles:r1333433106" about="#mwt62" typeof="mw:Extension/templatestyles mw:Transclusion" data-mw='{"name":"templatestyles","attrs":{"src":"Module:Citation/CS1/styles.css"},"body":{"extsrc":""},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"cite journal ","href":"./Template:Cite_journal"},"params":{"vauthors":{"wt":"Mulder CJ, van Wanrooij RL, Bakker SF, Wierdsma N, Bouma G"},"title":{"wt":"Gluten-free diet in gluten-related disorders"},"journal":{"wt":"Digestive Diseases"},"volume":{"wt":"31"},"issue":{"wt":"1"},"pages":{"wt":"57–62"},"date":{"wt":"2013"},"pmid":{"wt":"23797124"},"doi":{"wt":"10.1159/000347180"},"type":{"wt":"Review"}},"i":0}}]}' id="mwBYc"/><cite id="mwBYg" class="citation journal cs1" about="#mwt62" data-x-id="CITEREFMuldervan_WanrooijBakkerWierdsma2013">Mulder CJ, van Wanrooij RL, Bakker SF, Wierdsma N, Bouma G (2013). "Gluten-free diet in gluten-related disorders". <i id="mwBYk">Digestive Diseases</i> (Review). <b id="mwBYo">31</b> (1): <span class="nowrap" id="mwBYs">57–</span>62. <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="./Doi_(identifier)" title="Doi (identifier)" class="mw-redirect" id="mwBYw">doi</a>:<a rel="mw:ExtLink nofollow" href="https://doi.org/10.1159%2F000347180" class="external text" id="mwBY0">10.1159/000347180</a>. <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="./PMID_(identifier)" title="PMID (identifier)" class="mw-redirect" id="mwBY4">PMID</a><span typeof="mw:Entity" id="mwBY8"> </span><a rel="mw:ExtLink nofollow" href="https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/23797124" class="external text" id="mwBZA">23797124</a>.</cite><span title="ctx_ver=Z39.88-2004&amp;rft_val_fmt=info%3Aofi%2Ffmt%3Akev%3Amtx%3Ajournal&amp;rft.genre=article&amp;rft.jtitle=Digestive+Diseases&amp;rft.atitle=Gluten-free+diet+in+gluten-related+disorders&amp;rft.volume=31&amp;rft.issue=1&amp;rft.pages=57-62&amp;rft.date=2013&amp;rft_id=info%3Adoi%2F10.1159%2F000347180&amp;rft_id=info%3Apmid%2F23797124&amp;rft.aulast=Mulder&amp;rft.aufirst=CJ&amp;rft.au=van+Wanrooij%2C+RL&amp;rft.au=Bakker%2C+SF&amp;rft.au=Wierdsma%2C+N&amp;rft.au=Bouma%2C+G&amp;rfr_id=info%3Asid%2Fen.wikipedia.org%3AGluten-free+diet" class="Z3988" about="#mwt62" id="mwBZE"></span></span></li>
<li about="#cite_note-ElChammasDanner2011Quotation-12" id="cite_note-ElChammasDanner2011Quotation-12" data-mw-footnote-number="12"><span class="mw-cite-backlink" id="mwBZI"><a href="./Gluten-free_diet#cite_ref-ElChammasDanner2011Quotation_12-0" rel="mw:referencedBy" id="mwBZM"><span class="mw-linkback-text" id="mwBZQ">↑</span></a></span> <span id="mw-reference-text-cite_note-ElChammasDanner2011Quotation-12" class="mw-reference-text reference-text"><link rel="mw-deduplicated-inline-style" href="mw-data:TemplateStyles:r1333433106" about="#mwt31" typeof="mw:Extension/templatestyles mw:Transclusion" data-mw='{"name":"templatestyles","attrs":{"src":"Module:Citation/CS1/styles.css"},"body":{"extsrc":""},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"cite journal ","href":"./Template:Cite_journal"},"params":{"vauthors":{"wt":"El-Chammas K, Danner E"},"title":{"wt":"Gluten-free diet in nonceliac disease"},"journal":{"wt":"Nutrition in Clinical Practice"},"volume":{"wt":"26"},"issue":{"wt":"3"},"pages":{"wt":"294–9"},"date":{"wt":"June 2011"},"pmid":{"wt":"21586414"},"doi":{"wt":"10.1177/0884533611405538"},"type":{"wt":"Review"},"quote":{"wt":"The prescription of a GFD has been recommended for patients with IBS-like symptoms without histologic evidence of CD and who have positive IgA tTG antibodies or have the at-risk haplotypes DQ2 or DQ8.46 (...) Historically, a GFD was occasionally used in the management of multiple sclerosis (MS), because anecdotal reports indicated a positive effect (reversal of symptoms) of a GFD in MS patients. (…) what has been demonstrated so far is that a glutenfree vegan diet for 1 year significantly reduced disease activity and levels of antibodies to β-lactoglobulin and gliadin in patients with RA. (...) The beneficial effect of a GFD on diarrhea and weight gain in patients with HIV enteropathy has been demonstrated in a few case series. Treatment with a GFD has been observed to decrease the frequency of diarrhea and thus allow weight gain.84 &apos;&apos;(IBS=irritable bowel syndrome; RA=rheumatoid arthritis; GFD=gluten-free diet)&apos;&apos;"}},"i":0}}]}' id="mwBZU"/><cite id="CITEREFEl-ChammasDanner2011" class="citation journal cs1" about="#mwt31">El-Chammas K, Danner E (June 2011). "Gluten-free diet in nonceliac disease". <i id="mwBZY">Nutrition in Clinical Practice</i> (Review). <b id="mwBZc">26</b> (3): <span class="nowrap" id="mwBZg">294–</span>9. <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="./Doi_(identifier)" title="Doi (identifier)" class="mw-redirect" id="mwBZk">doi</a>:<a rel="mw:ExtLink nofollow" href="https://doi.org/10.1177%2F0884533611405538" class="external text" id="mwBZo">10.1177/0884533611405538</a>. <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="./PMID_(identifier)" title="PMID (identifier)" class="mw-redirect" id="mwBZs">PMID</a><span typeof="mw:Entity" id="mwBZw"> </span><a rel="mw:ExtLink nofollow" href="https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/21586414" class="external text" id="mwBZ0">21586414</a>. <q id="mwBZ4">The prescription of a GFD has been recommended for patients with IBS-like symptoms without histologic evidence of CD and who have positive IgA tTG antibodies or have the at-risk haplotypes DQ2 or DQ8.46 (...) Historically, a GFD was occasionally used in the management of multiple sclerosis (MS), because anecdotal reports indicated a positive effect (reversal of symptoms) of a GFD in MS patients. (…) what has been demonstrated so far is that a glutenfree vegan diet for 1 year significantly reduced disease activity and levels of antibodies to β-lactoglobulin and gliadin in patients with RA. (...) The beneficial effect of a GFD on diarrhea and weight gain in patients with HIV enteropathy has been demonstrated in a few case series. Treatment with a GFD has been observed to decrease the frequency of diarrhea and thus allow weight gain.84 <i id="mwBZ8">(IBS=irritable bowel syndrome; RA=rheumatoid arthritis; GFD=gluten-free diet)</i></q></cite><span title="ctx_ver=Z39.88-2004&amp;rft_val_fmt=info%3Aofi%2Ffmt%3Akev%3Amtx%3Ajournal&amp;r