<li about="#cite_note-RossiDiLollo-44" id="cite_note-RossiDiLollo-44" data-mw-footnote-number="44"><span class="mw-cite-backlink" id="mwB_g"><a href="./Gluten-free_diet#cite_ref-RossiDiLollo_44-0" rel="mw:referencedBy" id="mwB_k"><span class="mw-linkback-text" id="mwB_o">↑</span></a></span> <span id="mw-reference-text-cite_note-RossiDiLollo-44" class="mw-reference-text reference-text"><link rel="mw-deduplicated-inline-style" href="mw-data:TemplateStyles:r1333433106" about="#mwt149" typeof="mw:Extension/templatestyles mw:Transclusion" data-mw='{"name":"templatestyles","attrs":{"src":"Module:Citation/CS1/styles.css"},"body":{"extsrc":""},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"cite journal ","href":"./Template:Cite_journal"},"params":{"vauthors":{"wt":"Rossi A, Di Lollo AC, Guzzo MP, Giacomelli C, Atzeni F, Bazzichi L, Di Franco M"},"title":{"wt":"Fibromyalgia and nutrition: what news?"},"journal":{"wt":"Clinical and Experimental Rheumatology"},"volume":{"wt":"33"},"issue":{"wt":"1 Suppl 88"},"pages":{"wt":"S117-25"},"date":{"wt":"2015"},"pmid":{"wt":"25786053"},"type":{"wt":"Review"}},"i":0}}]}' id="mwB_s"/><cite id="CITEREFRossiDi_LolloGuzzoGiacomelli2015" class="citation journal cs1" about="#mwt149">Rossi A, Di Lollo AC, Guzzo MP, Giacomelli C, Atzeni F, Bazzichi L, Di Franco M (2015). "Fibromyalgia and nutrition: what news?". <i id="mwB_w">Clinical and Experimental Rheumatology</i> (Review). <b id="mwB_0">33</b> (1 Suppl 88): S117-25. <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="./PMID_(identifier)" title="PMID (identifier)" class="mw-redirect" id="mwB_4">PMID</a><span typeof="mw:Entity" id="mwB_8"> </span><a rel="mw:ExtLink nofollow" href="https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/25786053" class="external text" id="mwCAA">25786053</a>.</cite><span title="ctx_ver=Z39.88-2004&amp;rft_val_fmt=info%3Aofi%2Ffmt%3Akev%3Amtx%3Ajournal&amp;rft.genre=article&amp;rft.jtitle=Clinical+and+Experimental+Rheumatology&amp;rft.atitle=Fibromyalgia+and+nutrition%3A+what+news%3F&amp;rft.volume=33&amp;rft.issue=1+Suppl+88&amp;rft.pages=S117-25&amp;rft.date=2015&amp;rft_id=info%3Apmid%2F25786053&amp;rft.aulast=Rossi&amp;rft.aufirst=A&amp;rft.au=Di+Lollo%2C+AC&amp;rft.au=Guzzo%2C+MP&amp;rft.au=Giacomelli%2C+C&amp;rft.au=Atzeni%2C+F&amp;rft.au=Bazzichi%2C+L&amp;rft.au=Di+Franco%2C+M&amp;rfr_id=info%3Asid%2Fen.wikipedia.org%3AGluten-free+diet" class="Z3988" about="#mwt149" id="mwCAE"></span></span></li>
<li about="#cite_note-FasanoSapone2015Quotation2-45" id="cite_note-FasanoSapone2015Quotation2-45" data-mw-footnote-number="45"><span class="mw-cite-backlink" id="mwCAI"><a href="./Gluten-free_diet#cite_ref-FasanoSapone2015Quotation2_45-0" rel="mw:referencedBy" id="mwCAM"><span class="mw-linkback-text" id="mwCAQ">↑</span></a></span> <span id="mw-reference-text-cite_note-FasanoSapone2015Quotation2-45" class="mw-reference-text reference-text"><link rel="mw-deduplicated-inline-style" href="mw-data:TemplateStyles:r1333433106" about="#mwt164" typeof="mw:Extension/templatestyles mw:Transclusion" data-mw='{"name":"templatestyles","attrs":{"src":"Module:Citation/CS1/styles.css"},"body":{"extsrc":""},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"cite journal ","href":"./Template:Cite_journal"},"params":{"vauthors":{"wt":"Fasano A, Sapone A, Zevallos V, Schuppan D"},"title":{"wt":"Nonceliac gluten sensitivity"},"journal":{"wt":"Gastroenterology"},"volume":{"wt":"148"},"issue":{"wt":"6"},"pages":{"wt":"1195–204"},"date":{"wt":"May 2015"},"pmid":{"wt":"25583468"},"doi":{"wt":"10.1053/j.gastro.2014.12.049"},"type":{"wt":"Review"},"quote":{"wt":"One of the most controversial and highly debated discussions concerns the role of gluten in causing NCGS. Recent reports have indicated that gluten might not be the cause of NCGS, and some investigators still question whether NCGS as a real clinical entity. (...) Cereals such as wheat and rye, when consumed in normal quantities, are only minor sources of FODMAPs in the daily diet (Table 1). Therefore, gluten-containing grains are not likely to induce IBS exclusively via FODMAPs. In contrast, there is growing evidence that other proteins that are unique to gluten-containing cereals can elicit an innate immune response that leads to NCGS, raising a nomenclature issue. For this reason, wheat sensitivity, rather than gluten sensitivity, seems to be a more appropriate term, keeping in mind that other gluten-containing grains such as barley and rye also can trigger the symptoms."},"doi-access":{"wt":"free"}},"i":0}}]}' id="mwCAU"/><cite id="mwCAY" class="citation journal cs1" about="#mwt164" data-x-id="CITEREFFasanoSaponeZevallosSchuppan2015">Fasano A, Sapone A, Zevallos V, Schuppan D (May 2015). <a rel="mw:ExtLink nofollow" href="https://doi.org/10.1053%2Fj.gastro.2014.12.049" class="external text" id="mwCAc">"Nonceliac gluten sensitivity"</a>. <i id="mwCAg">Gastroenterology</i> (Review). <b id="mwCAk">148</b> (6): <span class="nowrap" id="mwCAo">1195–</span>204. <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="./Doi_(identifier)" title="Doi (identifier)" class="mw-redirect" id="mwCAs">doi</a>:<span class="id-lock-free" title="Freely accessible" id="mwCAw"><a rel="mw:ExtLink nofollow" href="https://doi.org/10.1053%2Fj.gastro.2014.12.049" class="external text" id="mwCA0">10.1053/j.gastro.2014.12.049</a></span>. <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="./PMID_(identifier)" title="PMID (identifier)" class="mw-redirect" id="mwCA4">PMID</a><span typeof="mw:Entity" id="mwCA8"> </span><a rel="mw:ExtLink nofollow" href="https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/25583468" class="external text" id="mwCBA">25583468</a>. <q id="mwCBE">One of the most controversial and highly debated discussions concerns the role of gluten in causing NCGS. Recent reports have indicated that gluten might not be the cause of NCGS, and some investigators still question whether NCGS as a real clinical entity. (...) Cereals such as wheat and rye, when consumed in normal quantities, are only minor sources of FODMAPs in the daily diet (Table 1). Therefore, gluten-containing grains are not likely to induce IBS exclusively via FODMAPs. In contrast, there is growing evidence that other proteins that are unique to gluten-containing cereals can elicit an innate immune response that leads to NCGS, raising a nomenclature issue. For this reason, wheat sensitivity, rather than gluten sensitivity, seems to be a more appropriate term, keeping in mind that other gluten-containing grains such as barley and rye also can trigger the symptoms.</q></cite><span title="ctx_ver=Z39.88-2004&amp;rft_val_fmt=info%3Aofi%2Ffmt%3Akev%3Amtx%3Ajournal&amp;rft.genre=article&amp;rft.jtitle=Gastroenterology&amp;rft.atitle=Nonceliac+gluten+sensitivity&amp;rft.volume=148&amp;rft.issue=6&amp;rft.pages=1195-204&amp;rft.date=2015-05&amp;rft_id=info%3Adoi%2F10.1053%2Fj.gastro.2014.12.049&amp;rft_id=info%3Apmid%2F25583468&amp;rft.aulast=Fasano&amp;rft.aufirst=A&amp;rft.au=Sapone%2C+A&amp;rft.au=Zevallos%2C+V&amp;rft.au=Schuppan%2C+D&amp;rft_id=https%3A%2F%2Fdoi.org%2F10.1053%252Fj.gastro.2014.12.049&amp;rfr_id=info%3Asid%2Fen.wikipedia.org%3AGluten-free+diet" class="Z3988" about="#mwt164" id="mwCBI"></span></span></li>
